advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_state
description   "Print the proof/theory state of a REPL at a given
              index: 0 = the base state (right after init), N = the
              state after step N-1, -1 = the latest state. Use it to
              re-read the current goal without re-running anything,
              or to inspect an earlier state before repl_fork /
              repl_truncate."
              (source: mcp_server.py state :331.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {
                 "repl": {"type": "string"},
                 "state_idx": {"type": "integer"}},
               "required": ["repl", "state_idx"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": true, "idempotentHint": true,
               "openWorldHint": false}
              (part of the readOnlyHint spec refinement flagged in
              plans/repl_list: Ir.state is the_repl + pretty_state,
              no claim, no mutation.)
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_state", {"repl":"T","state_idx":-1}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("state", [(repl,T),(state_idx,"-1")])
  ml dispatcher     "state" -> Ir.state (get "repl") (get_int "state_idx")
  engine            ir.ML:486 — negative idx = n+1+idx; out
                    (pretty_state st); out of range -> error

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [this tool] builtin row + handler; errors -> isError.
2. tests; rebuild per rules.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  index arithmetic matches the description: on a repl with 2 steps,
    valid indices are 0, 1, 2 and -1..-3; -1 equals 2.
    test [ml unit]: assert state at -1 and at n print identical
    output; 0 prints the base; n+1 and -(n+2) -> "out of range
    (0..n)" errors. (the missing-key and garbage-value cases are
    already in the build-time self-test from the I2 migration —
    s8/s9.)
T2  reading a state does not disturb the repl: no claim taken, works
    while another repl is busy, repeatable.
    test [ml unit]: state twice -> identical output; step still works.
    test [bridge]: state on B while A runs a slow step -> returns
    immediately (shared with plans/repl_step T5).
T3  full chain.
    test [e2e]: after two steps, repl_state -1 shows the goal text
    the model just saw from repl_step.

